<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eight Deer Jaguar Claw</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1063">1063</a>–<a href="/years/1115">1115</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Lord Eight Deer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eight Deer Jaguar Claw was a Mixtec ruler whose life is told in pictorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-zouche-nuttall">Codex Zouche-Nuttall</a>. Born into a noble family linked to Tilantongo, he established himself at Tututepec on the coast around 1083 and then won Tilantongo, uniting much of the Mixteca through conquest and marriage alliances. He was captured and sacrificed by his enemies in 1115.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eight-deer-jaguar-claw.jpg" alt="Eight Deer Jaguar Claw" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: An author from the Mixteca region c.1500 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Zouche-Nuttall_-_Lord_8_Deer_Lord_4_Jaguar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eight Deer, in Mixtec Iya Nacuaa Teyusi Ñaña, took his name from the date of his birth in the Mixtec calendar, 5 October 1063; his second name is also translated Tiger-Claw or Ocelot-Claw. His life is told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-zouche-nuttall">Codex Zouche-Nuttall</a>, a deerskin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the 15th century, and in other Mixtec manuscripts. He was the son of Five Alligator Sun Rain, high priest of Tilantongo, and Eleven Water Bird Jewel. Two of his brothers, Twelve Earthquake Bloody Jaguar and Nine Flower Copal Ball with Arrow, were his faithful companions in war.</p><p>His half-sister Six Lizard Jade Fan was at first his fiancée and lover, but she married his chief enemy, Eleven Wind Bloody Jaguar, king of the city called Xipe's Bundle or Red and White Bundle. Its lords had rights to the throne of Tilantongo and were the most important rivals to his power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Eight Deer is remembered for his conquests: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-zouche-nuttall">Codex Zouche-Nuttall</a> counts 94 cities taken during his reign, and he is almost always shown in a jaguar helmet. He supported Four Jaguar Face of the Night, the Toltec ruler of Cholula, in his expansion, and was rewarded with a turquoise nose ornament, a sign of Toltec royal authority. The scholar Maarten Jansen identifies Four Jaguar with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl">Ce Acatl Topiltzin</a>.</p><p>His marriages appear to have been a strategy for dominance through alliance with several royal lineages. In 1103 he married Thirteen Serpent Flowered Serpent, daughter of Six Lizard and Eleven Wind, and in 1105 three more wives, among them Ten Vulture Brilliant Quechquemitl, an heiress of Tilantongo. He conquered Xipe's Bundle, killed Eleven Wind and tortured and killed his brothers-in-law, sparing only the youngest, Four Wind. His son Six House Jaguar that Came from the Sky inherited Tilantongo and another son, Four Dog Coyote Hunter, Teozacoalco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Eight Deer was the only Mixtec king to unite kingdoms of all three Mixtec areas: Tilantongo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixteca-alta">Mixteca Alta</a>, Teozacoalco in the Mixteca Baja and Tututepec on the coast. His reputation as a great ruler made him a figure of legend among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtecs</a>, and parts of his story in the codices seem to merge with myth.</p><p>Knowledge of his life is limited because the codices are still not fully understood, and much of what is told of him rests on the interpretation of the codex by the Mixtec specialist John Pohl. As now understood, it is the tragic story of a man who achieved greatness and fell victim to his own hunger for power. Four Wind later married two of his daughters, Ten Flower Rain Spiderweb and Five Wind Fur and Jade Ornament, which legitimised his hold on Tututepec.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eight Deer Jaguar Claw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 11th century <b>Lord Eight Deer Jaguar Claw</b>, a lord from Tilantongo, built a wide domain through war and alliance before being sacrificed in 1115.</blockquote><a href="/state/tilantongo">Tilantongo</a></li><li><blockquote>Tututepec, Yucu Dzaa in Mixtec, became a royal seat in the late 11th century, when <b>Lord Eight Deer Jaguar Claw</b> established himself there around 1083 and used it as a base for conquests.</blockquote><a href="/state/tututepec">Tututepec</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tilantongo, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.25,-97.28&amp;year=1096" aria-label="Tilantongo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1906 1523 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eight Deer Jaguar Claw"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2356 1793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2356 1793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.25,-97.28&amp;year=1096">What was here in 1096? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
